"h350": "Specifications",
"h351": "What it needs and what it opens.",
"h36": "mTLS",
"h37": "Next to Graylog",
"h38": "Most sites run jt-glogarch on the Graylog VM. Exports and imports watch Graylog's heap, journal and buffers and this host's free memory, and slow down or pause before the box runs short; the dashboard says what size the VM should be.",
"h39": "On its own host",
"h4": "one VM (the common case)",
"h40": "Run it on a separate VM and point the archive path at NAS or NFS storage. It reads Graylog through the REST API, or OpenSearch directly (about five times faster), over the network.",
"h41": "Graylog 7 Data Node (beta)",
"h42": "A Data Node accepts only client certificates. <i>Connect Data Node</i> asks Graylog's own CA for one; jt-glogarch renews it before it expires, so OpenSearch Direct export and Bulk import work as on a plain OpenSearch.",
"h43": "Three ways to deploy",
"h44": "One Python service with its own web interface, scheduler and SQLite database. It needs to reach Graylog, and OpenSearch for the fast paths; nothing is installed on the Graylog nodes.",
"h45": "Graylog Open 6.x or 7.x that must keep logs longer than its hot indices do (regulation, forensics, contracts)",
"h46": "Old logs must go back into a Graylog to be searched again, with Graylog's own processing or exactly as they were",
"h47": "Administrators' actions in Graylog must be recorded where Graylog admins cannot delete them",
"h48": "You want security incidents, Sigma rules and AI chat on top of Graylog Open, with your own LLM",
"h49": "Graylog Enterprise's Archiving already covers what you need: use it",
"h5": "jt-glogarch",
"h50": "Analysing archives in place: archives have no index. Record search scans files for a keyword; real analysis means importing the period back",
"h51": "Graylog 5 or older: measured on Graylog 6.3 to 7.2 only",
"h52": "A complete commercial SIEM: detections are Graylog aggregations, and what they see depends on how your logs are parsed",
"h53": "A good fit",
"h54": "Not a fit (today)",
"h55": "Where it fits",
"h56": "jt-glogarch sits beside Graylog and talks to it through its API and OpenSearch; it never changes how Graylog ingests logs.",
"h57": "Six uses for one archive",
"h58": "Once logs are compressed files with checksums, they serve every one of these.",
"h59": "Keep logs for compliance",
"h6": "web UI · scheduler",
"h60": "A policy asks for a year of authentication logs while Graylog keeps 90 days hot: a daily schedule archives the streams that matter to cheap storage.",
"h61": "Forensics",
"h62": "An incident from six months ago needs a look, long after OpenSearch dropped it: find the hours in the archive list, import, search again.",
"h63": "Cluster migration",
"h64": "Move from an old Graylog to a new one: OpenSearch Direct exports the old cluster fast, GELF or Bulk loads the new one. No cloud in between.",
"h65": "Off-site copy",
"h66": "Mount remote storage as the archive path and schedule a daily export: searchable history survives the loss of the main cluster.",
"h67": "Smaller hot tier",
"h68": "Archives are about ten times smaller than indexed data, so the live cluster keeps only recent days and older data is minutes away.",
"h69": "Independent audit",
"h7": "Archive disk",
"h70": "The operation audit records what administrators really did in Graylog, in a database Graylog admins cannot touch.",
"h71": "Two ways out",
"h72": "Graylog REST API (works on any Graylog, stream filters) or OpenSearch Direct (about 5× faster, no 10,000-result window).",
"h73": "Two ways back",
"h74": "GELF replays every message through Graylog's inputs, pipelines and streams; OpenSearch Bulk writes them as they were, 5–10× faster.",
"h75": "Streamed to disk",
"h76": "Records go to <code>.json.gz</code> as they arrive and never pile up in memory; files roll over at 50 MB.",
"h77": "No duplicates, no gaps",
"h78": "Time already archived is never fetched again, whichever mode archived it; a scheduled run first refills holes left by earlier runs.",
"h79": "Resumes where it stopped",
"h8": "Graylog",
"h80": "A restart or a cancel keeps every finished hour and discards only the hour in progress; the next run continues from there.",
"h81": "SHA256 and HMAC",
"h82": "Every archive has a SHA256 checksum, re-verified on a schedule; an optional keyed HMAC and an off-box ledger catch a forged file-and-database pair.",
"h83": "Cron schedules",
"h84": "Export, cleanup and verify on standard POSIX cron. Changes in the web UI apply at once and every run shows in the job history.",
"h85": "Backs off when Graylog is busy",
"h86": "Heap after garbage collection, journal and buffers are read every 15 seconds; exports slow down, pause, and stop once rather than wedge Graylog.",
"h87": "Preflight before import",
"h88": "Target capacity, retention and field-type conflicts are checked and fixed before the first message; afterwards indexer failures are reconciled.",
"h89": "Retention and disk outlook",
"h9": "OpenSearch",
"h90": "Cleanup keeps what the retention says and never deletes a file still being written; the dashboard estimates how many months the disk still holds.",
"h91": "Record search",
"h92": "Find records inside archives without restoring them: keyword and field conditions, hits highlighted, CSV or JSON Lines download.",
"h93": "Alerting in bulk",
"h94": "Attach or detach notifications on many Graylog event definitions at once, as the signed-in user, with a preview and undo.",
"h95": "Eight notification channels",
"h96": "Telegram, Discord, Slack, Microsoft Teams, Nextcloud Talk, Zulip, e-mail and a standard webhook signed with HMAC, in English, Traditional Chinese or Japanese.",
"h97": "Web UI and CLI",
"h98": "Light and dark themes in three languages, plus the <code>glogarch</code> command for scripts; both use the same database.",
"h99": "Sized from this host",
